(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-pd2-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"PD2 Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which abbreviation refers to the RIGHT eye, and which to both?","choices":["OD is the left eye; OS is the right","OD is the right eye; OU is both eyes","OS is the right eye; OD is both eyes","OU is the right eye; OS is both eyes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Beck said these abbreviations are terms you must remember. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is microtia?","choices":["A congenitally underdeveloped","An absent or closed ear canal","An extra accessory auricle","A small pit anterior to the tragus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is grouped with atresia among the congenital deformities. Abnormally small auricle. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Acute, unilateral and PAINFUL visual loss localises to where, and includes which causes?","choices":["Usually the cornea and anterior chamber","Usually the retina — vascular occlusion and detachment","Usually the optic nerve — optic neuritis alone","Usually the lids — blepharitis and hordeolum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is both the location and the list. Corneal abrasion or ulcer, uveitis, traumatic hyphaema, acute narrow angle glaucoma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why should dentures be removed during the oral examination?","choices":["To let the patient speak more clearly during the history","To look underneath for ulcers and lesions","To assess the fit of the denture itself","To allow the tongue blade to be inserted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A denture hides exactly the mucosa most at risk from chronic irritation. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which immunisation status matters in eye trauma?","choices":["Pneumococcal","Hepatitis B","Tetanus","Influenza"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is tetanus status specifically for eye trauma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which gland surrounds the hair follicle and keeps hair and skin moist?","choices":["The eccrine gland","The apocrine gland","The sudoriferous gland","The sebaceous gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it secretes sebum directly into the follicle. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as CENTRAL rather than peripheral?","choices":["Aural fullness with fluctuating tinnitus","Hearing unaffected, tinnitus absent","Hearing impaired on one side with tinnitus present","Episodes lasting under a minute triggered by head position"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The absence of ear symptoms alongside the presence of neurological ones is the pattern. Other brainstem deficits such as dysarthria, ataxia or crossed motor and sensory deficits. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which compensatory finding accompanies diplopia?","choices":["A compensatory squint of one eye only","Compensatory pupil dilation","Compensatory tearing","A compensatory head posture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — patients tilt or turn to fuse the images. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does an acuity of 20/200 mean?","choices":["The patient has 200 degrees of visual field at 20 feet","At 20 feet the patient reads print that a person with normal vision could read at 200 feet","At 200 feet the patient reads print that a normal eye reads at 20 feet","The patient sees 20 per cent of normal at 200 feet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and the larger the second number, the worse the vision. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What are exostoses, and which type of hearing loss do they cause?","choices":["Benign bony growths in the ear canal, causing conductive loss","Benign bony growths on the ossicles, causing sensorineural loss","Malignant bone tumours of the temporal bone","Cholesterol deposits in the middle ear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They narrow the canal mechanically, which is why the loss is conductive. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which findings belong in an oral case presentation?","choices":["Only the abnormal findings, since normal findings add nothing","Only the physical examination findings, since the history is documented separately","Pertinent positives and negatives from both the history and the physical examination","Every finding obtained, whether positive or negative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Choosing what counts as pertinent is the clinical reasoning being assessed. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"How is the tongue palpated?","choices":["Palpate with both hands simultaneously from below the jaw","Ask the patient to protrude the tongue and palpate the tip only","Use a tongue blade to depress and palpate through it","Hold it with gauze in one hand and palpate with the other"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Switching hands is what lets you reach both lateral borders properly, and the lateral border is where cancers sit. Then switch hands for the opposite side. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary skin lesion from a secondary one?","choices":["A primary lesion is the one the patient noticed first","A primary lesion forms first and results directly from the disease","A primary lesion is larger than one centimetre and a secondary lesion is smaller","A primary lesion is flat and a secondary lesion is elevated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Identifying the primary lesion is the key to interpreting and describing the eruption. While a secondary lesion is a change in that lesion over time. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Dome-shaped, bright red to violet or black lesions also known as Campbell De Morgan spots are called what?","choices":["Spider angiomas","Telangiectasias","Ecchymoses","Cherry angiomas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they may or may not blanch. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which historical factors should be explored for their relationship to the appearance of skin lesions?","choices":["The patient's blood type and immunisation record","Season, travel, heat or cold, previous reactions, drugs and menses","Season and travel only, since the remainder are rarely relevant","The patient's height, weight and body mass index"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — each can be the trigger that explains the timing of an eruption. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What distinguishes a bulla from a vesicle?","choices":["A bulla contains purulent material and a vesicle contains clear fluid","A bulla is deeper in the dermis than a vesicle","A bulla is solid and a vesicle is fluid-filled","A bulla is larger than one centimetre and a vesicle is smaller"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both are superficial elevations filled with fluid, and only size separates them. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A patient reports ear pain but the ear itself looks entirely normal. Which structures should the history now cover?","choices":["The thyroid gland and the trachea","The parotid and submandibular glands","The temporomandibular joint","The frontal and maxillary sinuses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ear pain is frequently referred, and these three are the usual sources. The first answer a patient gives is not the last question you ask. The teeth and the cervical spine. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why did Beck ask the class to know the referral list specifically?","choices":["Because it appears in the textbook","Because they will be the ones making those dispositions","Because it closes the topic","Because it duplicates PD1 content"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — her stated reason: 'you're going to be making those dispos'. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"What is chemosis?","choices":["Forward displacement of the eye in the orbit","Outward turning of the lower eyelid","Swelling and inflammation of the conjunctiva","Injection of the superficially visible vessels of the conjunctiva or sclera"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the definition. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In what order should an oral case presentation generally proceed?","choices":["In reverse, opening with the diagnosis and working back to the evidence","Grouped by body system in alphabetical order","Physical examination findings first, then the history that prompted them","Mostly the order in which the history was obtained and the examination performed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Following the encounter's own sequence is what makes it easy to follow. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 9"},{"q":"A non-blanching reddish-purple macule measuring six millimetres is called what?","choices":["Purpura","A petechia","An ecchymosis","A telangiectasia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the three millimetre to one centimetre range defines purpura. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"You lift a fold of skin and it does not lift with ease. What does reduced skin mobility suggest?","choices":["Recent sun exposure","Edema","Dehydration","Normal ageing of the skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fluid within the tissue tethers the skin and reduces how freely it moves. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Small depressions in the nail plate representing dystrophy of the plate are called what?","choices":["Nail pitting","Splinter hemorrhages","Beau's lines","Koilonychia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the pits are areas of small depressions in the nail plate. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What is the underlying lesion in Adie's tonic pupil?","choices":["Degeneration of the ciliary ganglia and the postganglionic parasympathetic fibres","Interruption of the sympathetic supply","Compression of the oculomotor nerve","Demyelination of the optic nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the stated pathology. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which finding defines a stage IV pressure ulcer?","choices":["Non-blanching erythema over intact skin","Full thickness skin loss with destruction of tissue, muscle and bone","Full thickness skin loss reaching but not penetrating muscle","Partial thickness loss of the epidermis and dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — involvement of muscle and bone is what separates stage IV from stage III. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which size of speculum should be used, and why?","choices":["A medium speculum regardless of canal size","Whichever gives the widest field of view, seal notwithstanding","The largest that will fit the canal","The smallest available, to reduce discomfort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A small speculum is tempting but it leaks, and without a seal the pneumatic test tells you nothing. Because insufflation needs a proper seal. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why does the ABSENCE of cough count toward the Centor score rather than its presence?","choices":["A cough points toward a viral cause","Coughing masks tonsillar exudates on examination","A cough indicates the infection has spread to the lower airway","Cough is a criterion only in children"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The rule is built to separate bacterial from viral pharyngitis, and cough is the most useful viral marker in the set. So its absence raises the probability of streptococcal infection. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What distinguishes a focused encounter from a comprehensive one?","choices":["The history and examination are both narrowed to what the presenting problem requires","The examination is narrowed but the history remains complete","The history is narrowed but every system is still examined","The encounter is identical but documented more briefly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a focused encounter is not a shorter version of everything, it is a targeted selection. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 7"},{"q":"In unilateral CONDUCTIVE loss, which way does the Weber sound lateralise, and why?","choices":["It does not lateralise in conductive loss","It alternates between ears","To the IMPAIRED ear","To the GOOD ear, because the impaired ear transmits nothing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The block screens out competing sound, leaving the bone-conducted tone unopposed in that ear. Because room noise is not well heard on that side so vibration is detected better. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A MULTINODULAR enlarged thyroid carries a risk of malignancy under what circumstance?","choices":["When there is a family history","Always, regardless of history","Only if the gland is tender","Only in patients under thirty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the multinodular gland is usually a metabolic process, and family history is what raises the concern. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is the normal appearance of the fundus and disc?","choices":["White with absent tiny disc vessels","An enlarged cup with vessels sinking in around the disc","Yellowish-orange to cream colour, small disc vessels, a sharp disc margin, and a cup located centrally or slightly temporal with a diameter normally less than half the disc","Pink with blurred margins and no visible cup"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the full normal description, including the half-disc cup rule. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What is the warning about pressure on a globe that may be ruptured?","choices":["Pressure should be applied only with a cotton bud","Failing to diagnose a penetrated or ruptured globe","Pressure is required to assess the depth of injury","Pressure is harmless once an anaesthetic is instilled"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is both the missed diagnosis and the pressure. Placing too much pressure on it, is a listed mistake. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are the pupil and lid findings in an oculomotor nerve palsy?","choices":["A small pupil that reacts briskly, with ptosis and anhidrosis","A large pupil with a slow but present near reaction","Small irregular pupils that accommodate but do not react","A dilated pupil fixed to both light and near effort"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the full picture. With ptosis and lateral deviation of the eye almost always present. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"How do multiple NEW flashes or floaters differ in significance from a single floater?","choices":["Both are always benign","Both always require emergency referral","Multiple new flashes or floaters suggest a retinal tear or vitreous haemorrhage","A single floater suggests a retinal tear; multiple floaters are benign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the number is what changes the meaning. A single floater is probably benign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A pressure ulcer shows full thickness skin loss with necrosis of subcutaneous tissue extending down to, but not through, the underlying muscle. What stage is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage III","Stage II","Stage IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — reaching muscle without destroying it is exactly the stage III boundary. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Vertigo comes on when the patient rolls onto one side in bed, lasts under a minute, and hearing is unaffected. Which is it?","choices":["Vestibular neuronitis","Acoustic neuroma","Benign positional vertigo","Ménière disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sudden onset on rolling onto the affected side or tilting the head up, a few seconds to under a minute, hearing not affected, tinnitus absent. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does a septal haematoma require URGENT drainage?","choices":["To prevent the blood tracking into the orbit","To prevent airway obstruction","To prevent secondary sinusitis","To prevent necrosis of the septal cartilage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cartilage has no blood supply of its own — it depends on the lining that the haematoma has stripped away, so the clock starts immediately. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, how is the PAIN of acute iritis described?","choices":["Moderate, aching, deep","Mild discomfort rather than pain","Severe, aching, deep","Absent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the chart's iritis column. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"The Weber test is done and the patient hears the tone only in the LEFT ear. Which ear has sensorineural hearing loss?","choices":["The left","Neither — this is a normal result","Both","The right"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sensorineural loss lateralises AWAY from the damaged side, so hearing the tone on the left places the lesion on the right. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Where do xanthelasma plaques sit, and what should they prompt?","choices":["On the bulbar conjunctiva — consider ultraviolet exposure","Along the NASAL portions of one or both eyelids — consider lipid disorders","Along the temporal portions of the lids — consider thyroid disease","At the lid margin — consider bacterial infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is both the position and the inference. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Besides non-blanching erythema, which changes characterise a stage I pressure ulcer?","choices":["Partial loss of the epidermis with a moist base","Necrosis extending into subcutaneous tissue","Changes in temperature","Changes in temperature alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the area may be warm or cool, firm or boggy, and painful or itchy. Consistency, sensation and colour. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which of the following is a recognised cause of onycholysis?","choices":["Prolonged immersion of the hands in water","Iron deficiency producing a thinned nail plate","Chronic hypoxia from lung disease","A systemic illness three months previously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — local irritation from chemical exposure or prolonged immersion is one cause, alongside fungal infection, psoriasis, medications such as tetracycline, and trauma. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What are the four Centor criteria?","choices":["Fever, tonsillar exudates, swollen anterior cervical adenopathy, and ABSENCE of cough","Fever, tonsillar exudates, swollen anterior cervical adenopathy, and PRESENCE of cough","Fever, sore throat, headache and myalgia","Fever, trismus, uvular deviation and drooling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three are things present and one is a thing absent, which is the part most often misremembered. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which skin symptoms should be asked about when taking the history?","choices":["Fever, chills and night sweats","Erythema, scaling and crusting","Pruritus, pain and paresthesia","Pruritus alone, since it is the commonest skin symptom"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — itch, pain and abnormal sensation are the three symptom categories. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which part of the examiner's hand should rest against the patient during otoscopy, and what does it achieve?","choices":["The ulnar aspect","The palm, to steady the patient's head","The thumb, to hold the auricle in position","No part — the otoscope is held clear of the patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The hand braces against the patient's head, so the instrument moves with the patient rather than into them. Which anchors the otoscope so a sudden movement does not drive it into the canal. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does a NORMAL lymph node feel like?","choices":["Hard, fixed and irregular","Soft, tender and warm","Rubbery, matted and non-tender","Round or ovoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four features matter, because each abnormal finding is a departure from one of them. Smooth, mobile and non-tender. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A patient has bluish discoloration of the fingers and toes with a normal oxygen level. What does peripheral cyanosis usually reflect?","choices":["Excess melanin production in the extremities","Chronic exposure to cold water","Inadequate circulation","Inadequate oxygenation in the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — peripheral cyanosis is a perfusion problem, which is why it spares the central mucous membranes. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A patient has central cyanosis. What is the usual explanation?","choices":["Increased pigmentation of the skin","Deposition of bilirubin in the skin","Inadequate oxygenation in the lungs","Inadequate circulation to the extremities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — central cyanosis points to a pulmonary problem rather than a circulatory one. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"On otoscopy, which type of hearing loss usually shows a visible abnormality — and what is the exception?","choices":["Both are usually visible","Conductive loss is always visible with no exception","Conductive loss is usually visible, except in otosclerosis","Sensorineural loss is usually visible; conductive loss is not"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis is the conductive cause hiding behind a normal-looking drum, which is exactly why it catches people out. Sensorineural loss is not visible. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"When must the lid never be everted?","choices":["If a foreign body has already been seen","If rupture of the globe is suspected","If the patient has conjunctivitis","If the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the material states this as an absolute. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Scaliness of the eyebrows suggests what?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Contact dermatitis","Psoriasis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the association for eyebrow scaling. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A nail whose proximal portion is white and whose distal portion is dark is described as what?","choices":["Mee's lines","Beau's lines","Splinter hemorrhages","Terry's nails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the proximal white and distal dark pattern defines the finding. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A reddish-purple, non-blanching macule measuring two millimetres is called what?","choices":["An ecchymosis","A cherry angioma","A petechia","Purpura"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — petechiae are under three millimetres. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient's own voice is UNUSUALLY LOUD. Which kind of hearing loss does that suggest, and why?","choices":["Neither — loud voice indicates a psychiatric cause","Sensorineural","Conductive — the middle ear amplifies the patient's own voice","Either — voice volume does not distinguish them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Voice volume is a feedback loop: the worse you hear yourself, the louder you speak. The patient cannot hear themselves well, so they raise their voice. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which bacteria are named as the common causes of otitis media?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans","Streptococcus pyogenes and Moraxella lacunata"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the two organisms named for this finding, and both are respiratory tract commensals that reach the middle ear through the Eustachian tube. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which pupil findings are abnormal?","choices":["Any pupil under 4 mm","Any pupil that constricts to light","Equal pupils that both react briskly","A difference in size greater than 1 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the two criteria. Or a poorly reactive pupil. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which four dimensions assess any eye complaint?","choices":["Onset, duration, severity and radiation","Location, quality, quantity and timing","Fever, discharge, photophobia and itch","Time course, precipitating factors, palliative or exacerbating variables, and vision loss or visual deficits"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the four-part frame for the history. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does moving the light to the affected eye cause BOTH pupils to dilate in an afferent defect?","choices":["The ciliary ganglion has degenerated","The afferent stimulus on that side is reduced","The affected pupil is pharmacologically blocked","The oculomotor nerve is compressed on that side"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the explanation of the mechanism. So the efferent signal to both pupils is reduced and a net dilation results. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"You press a piece of clear glass against a red lesion and the colour fades. What does this indicate?","choices":["Vascular engorgement","Hemorrhage into the skin","Increased melanin pigmentation","Deposition of bilirubin in the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In diascopy, blood that can be pressed out of vessels blanches; blood outside vessels cannot. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Hair loss caused by an urge to pull out one's own hair is called what?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Androgenic alopecia","Hirsutism","Trichotillomania"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — patches may be single or multiple and range from a few square centimetres to the entire scalp. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 80"}]};
